R = {}
R["«EXEC»"] = """*(Synthetic orders, returns and reviews with a hidden body-size and garment-fit model, built on the documented design of Flipkart's size and fit recommender: three-class labels from return reason codes, review text as an extra signal, four fashion categories. Flipkart's data, thresholds and business impact are not public, so this is an interview-standard solution on simulated data, not a reproduction of their system. Written after the analysis.)*

- **The question:** which orders should get a "size up / size down" tip, does review text improve the prediction, and how many ₹ crore does the tip save net of tips that backfire?
- **The label is noisy by construction.** Only 50% of truly mismatched orders were returned, so **5.2% of the "fit" class is really mismatched**, and 4.0% of size returns carry the wrong direction. Only 48% of true mismatches carry the right label.
- **History is the strongest signal.** For customers with a usual size, each step above it multiplies the odds of "too large" by **3.9** (95% CI 3.8 to 4.0) and cuts the odds of "too small" to **0.24**. Permuting the history and size-gap features costs 0.111 mAP, brand and fabric 0.088, reviews 0.029.
- **Reviews help, modestly.** Adding review features raises mean average precision (mAP) by **+0.008** for boosting (CI +0.004 to +0.011) and for logistic regression, and macro-F1 by about +0.005 (Holm p = 0.040). Boosting with reviews reaches mAP 0.175 against 0.167 without. The gain is small, as the paper also finds (1% to 4% relative).
- **Macro-F1 is a weak yardstick here.** Always predicting "fit" scores 0.325; logistic regression scores 0.323 and boosting 0.356 (CI 0.352 to 0.359). Ranking quality (mAP, calibration) shows the real signal: the top 1% of scores are right **36%** of the time against a base rate near 3%.
- **Reviews help only if the product has reviews.** The gain for products with 5+ informative reviews is **+0.014** (CI +0.006 to +0.023), with 1 to 4 reviews +0.016, with none **−0.0003** (no gain). For new customers the gain (+0.004, CI −0.001 to +0.009) is not significantly different from returning customers (p = 0.20), so the paper's cold-start motivation is not confirmed on this data.
- **Transfer needs platform data.** A classifier trained only on public English reviews has macro-F1 0.72 on platform fit tags against **0.84** when trained on platform tags (McNemar p < 1e-60); it fails on Hinglish.
- **Money (assumed costs, twin effect):** at the validation-chosen cut-off the tip is shown on **26%** of orders and, if followed, cuts size returns among them from 13.4% to 2.3%. Base case: about **₹3.7 crore a year** net of ₹1.2 crore running cost (2 crore orders, 30% follow, ₹380 per return). **Worst case −₹2.2 crore, best case +₹10.6 crore.** The sign depends on how many customers follow the tip.
- **A/B test:** analysing only flagged orders, a **20% relative drop** in their size-return rate (24% at a top-5% cut-off) needs about **1,100 flagged orders per arm** (23,000 orders, 825 customers); analysing all orders would need 120,000 per arm.
- **I recommend:** pilot the tip in two categories at a cut-off near the top 5% to 10% of orders, measure on triggered orders, and scale only if the A/B shows the follow rate and return drop the base case needs."""
R["«O5»"] = """the data has **1,500 duplicate orders**, **3,965 orders with no size**, **75 products with no brand**, **3,640 generic bulk reviews** (two words or fewer, repeated at least 100 times) and **959 size returns with the wrong direction code** (visible only because this is a simulation). No review is dated inside the order window, so reviews cannot leak the outcome. After cleaning there are **476,048 orders from 143,395 customers on 7,000 products**. The simulated class shares are within about 0.1 percentage point of the paper's Table 1 (for example Women Jacket small 4.43% vs 4.52% documented, large 1.75% vs 1.68%). In the hidden truth, **9.84% of orders are truly mismatched**, half were silently kept, so **5.2% of the "fit" class is in fact mismatched**."""
R["«O61»"] = """the shares match the documented ones, and cold start is common. **39.8% of orders come from new customers** with no history, 31.0% from customers with 1 to 2 prior orders and 29.2% from customers with 3 or more. **38.8% of orders are on products with no informative review**, 22.7% on products with 1 to 4 and 38.5% on products with 5 or more. A model that depends only on customer history or on reviews would be blind for a large share of orders."""
R["«O62»"] = """ordering off one's usual size is a strong, near-monotone signal. At the usual size, 1.3% of orders are returned as too small and 0.7% as too large. One step above the usual size, **4.0% are returned as too large** (and 0.3% as too small); two steps above, **12.3%**; three steps, 24.7%. One step below, **6.6% are returned as too small**; two steps, 17.6%; three steps, 25.4%. The ±3 buckets are small (about 650 to 700 orders each), so their rates are rough. This is the signal a new customer lacks."""
R["«O71»"] = """the class mix differs significantly across categories (chi-square 1,648, df 6, p < 1e-300), but the effect is small (Cramér's V = 0.042), so categories differ by a few tenths of a percentage point in mix. The noise is large: only **50.0% of truly mismatched orders were returned**, so **5.2% of the "fit" class is truly mismatched**, **4.0% of size returns have the wrong direction**, and the labels carry the right class for only **48.0% of true mismatches**. Every score below has a ceiling set by this noise. In real data the same check would need exchange records or a fit survey."""
R["«O72»"] = """for customers with a usual size, each step the ordered size is above it **multiplies the odds of "too small" by 0.24** (95% CI 0.23 to 0.25) and **the odds of "too large" by 3.89** (CI 3.77 to 4.02); both p < 1e-100, so we reject H1. The placebo for new customers (ordered size minus the category average) is weaker but still significant: odds ratios 0.48 (CI 0.47 to 0.49) for small and 2.17 (CI 2.10 to 2.23) for large. Ordering far from the category norm is more often a wrong guess; I did not test that explanation. The history signal is worth far more than the category-level proxy."""
R["«O73»"] = """a classifier trained only on the public English labelled set reaches macro-F1 **0.724** (95% CI 0.708 to 0.742) on the platform's tagged reviews, against **0.838** (CI 0.825 to 0.851) for the platform-trained one. They agree on English-style text (0.789 vs 0.788) and differ on Hinglish-style text (**0.40 vs 1.00**), which is 21.0% of tagged reviews. McNemar: the platform model alone is right on 366 reviews and the public model alone on 31 (p = 4.6e-63), so we reject H4. The 1.00 on Hinglish is an artefact of the simulation: the keyword that defines "Hinglish-style" appears only in reviews that state the fit in Hindi, and our templates are clean. Real Hinglish text is messier, so the gap is directionally right but exaggerated."""
R["«O74»"] = """for the 1,205 products with at least 30 training orders and 3 informative reviews, the review score (mean P(large) − mean P(small)) has a **Spearman correlation of +0.567** (95% CI +0.526 to +0.609) with the product's actual large-minus-small return rate. Using the hidden truth, the score tracks the true product offset better as reviews accumulate: correlation **0.44** with 1 to 2 informative reviews, 0.65 with 3 to 5, 0.78 with 6 to 10, 0.86 with 11 to 20 and **0.93** with 21 or more. So the score is useful but noisy for products with few reviews, which is why the model also sees the review count."""
R["«O75»"] = """the always-"fit" prior scores macro-F1 **0.325**. **Macro-F1 barely separates the models**: logistic regression without reviews is at 0.323 (CI 0.320 to 0.327), no better than the prior, because the class-balanced argmax predicts many false small and large. Boosting is higher: 0.351 without reviews (CI 0.348 to 0.355) and **0.356 with reviews** (0.352 to 0.359). The ranking metric shows more: mAP is 0.156 (logistic) and 0.167 (boosting) without reviews, and **0.164 and 0.175 with reviews**; random ranking would give about 0.025. Reviews alone score mAP 0.046 and macro-F1 0.214, far below the baseline, matching the paper's finding that review-only models lose. The public-trained review variant (G2) has mAP 0.172 and macro-F1 0.358. The paper's baseline macro-F1 (35.6 to 38.3) is on different data and is not comparable."""
R["«O76»"] = """**adding reviews raises mAP by +0.0084 for logistic regression** (CI +0.0055 to +0.0109) and **by +0.0082 for boosting** (CI +0.0037 to +0.0114), with macro-F1 gains of +0.0054 and +0.0045; the Holm-adjusted p is 0.040 for both (the bootstrap has 200 resamples, so the smallest possible p is 0.01 before correction). We reject H2, but the gain is small: under 5% relative in mAP. **Boosting beats logistic regression** by +0.011 mAP and +0.028 macro-F1. The platform-trained review features beat the public-trained ones by +0.0037 mAP (CI +0.0004 to +0.0070, Holm p = 0.050) but have a **lower macro-F1 by 0.0024** (CI −0.0036 to −0.0012), so the transfer comparison is mixed and borderline."""
R["«O77»"] = """**gain by customer type:** +0.0042 mAP for new customers (CI −0.0005 to +0.0092, includes zero) and +0.0100 for customers with history (CI +0.0041 to +0.0165). The difference (new minus returning) is −0.0058 (CI −0.0131 to +0.0020, p = 0.20), so we cannot reject H3 for customer type; on this data the paper's cold-start motivation is **not confirmed**. **Gain by review volume** is clear: **−0.0003** for products with 0 informative reviews (CI −0.0061 to +0.0049), **+0.0155** for 1 to 4 (CI +0.0058 to +0.0242) and **+0.0138** for 5+ (CI +0.0056 to +0.0226). Reviews cannot help a product that has none, which is the new-product cold start. By category only Men's Polo shows a gain near significance (+0.0147, Holm p = 0.053); jacket, kurta and jean gains are not distinguishable from zero after Holm."""
R["«O78»"] = """probabilities are well calibrated after Platt scaling on the validation period (ECE 0.0026 for small and 0.0022 for large), and the top decile of the score has a **lift of 5.5 for small and 6.0 for large** (observed rates 16.8% and 11.5% against base rates of 3.1% and 1.9%). Among the top 1% of orders, **36.1%** (CI 33.0% to 39.3%) have the predicted problem, a lift of 12.7; the top 5% have 24.3% (lift 8.8), the top 10% 19.7% and the top 20% 14.6% (lift 5.5). All are far above the base rate (one-sided p < 1e-4), so we reject H5. The more orders we flag, the lower the precision, which is why the tip cut-off is a business choice."""
R["«O79»"] = """flagging the top 5% of orders, the control rate of the predicted size problem is **24.3%**. To detect a **10% relative drop** needs 4,710 flagged orders per arm (94,195 orders, 3,432 customers); **20%** needs 1,131 (22,618 orders, 825 customers); **30%** needs 480 (9,599 orders, 350 customers). Ignoring triggering and analysing all orders would need 496,415, 122,646 and 53,860 orders per arm. Analysing only triggered orders, with a control group that would also have been flagged, cuts the sample needed by a factor of about five."""
R["«O8»"] = """when feature groups are permuted together, the largest drops in mAP are **customer history and size gap (0.111)**, **brand and fabric (0.088)**, **ordered size (0.065)** and then **review features (0.029)**. Category, price and rating (0.004) and customer demographics (about 0) add almost nothing. Reviews are fourth in importance and the only group that carries product-specific fit information on top of the brand, so they matter for products whose brand average hides a runs-small style."""
R["«O91»"] = """on the validation period, a low cut-off flags many orders and a high one flags few but accurate ones. The returns avoided per flagged order (if the tip is followed) rise from 0.081 at cut-off 0.04 (37% of orders flagged) to 0.259 at 0.30 (0.4% flagged), but net value per order peaks at **cut-off 0.06 (₹2.31 per order, 25.8% of orders flagged)** because value needs volume. This is the base-case optimum under assumed costs; a more cautious team may pick a higher cut-off to avoid showing tips on a quarter of orders."""
R["«O9»"] = """on the test period the tip is shown on **25.9% of orders** (22,968), with a direction precision of **12.7%**. If followed by everyone, the size-return rate among flagged orders falls from **13.4% to 2.3%**, or **0.111 returns avoided per tip** (95% CI 0.106 to 0.115). The gain per 100 tips is similar across categories: 11.8 for Women Jacket, 12.2 for Women Kurta and Kurti, 10.4 for Men's Jean and 10.1 for Men's Polo. At 2 crore orders a year the base case avoids about **1.73 lakh returns a year** and nets **₹3.7 crore** (twin-effect CI 3.4 to 3.9) after a ₹1.2 crore running cost. The **worst case loses ₹2.2 crore** (15% follow, ₹280 per return, 3% lost sales) and the best case gains **₹10.6 crore**. The CI covers only the twin's effect estimate, not the assumed costs and follow rate, which dominate the range. The twin assumes a customer who follows a tip moves exactly one size, which real customers may not."""
R["«O10»"] = """**Recommendation**

1. **Build the size tip as a calibrated three-class model** (boosting on customer history, size gap, brand, fabric and review features), recalibrated per period. Rank by calibrated probability, not by macro-F1.
2. **Pilot in two categories** (Men's Polo, where the review gain is largest, and Men's Jean) with a cut-off near the top 5% to 10% of orders (precision 20% to 24%), not the 26% value-optimal cut-off, to protect trust.
3. **Run the A/B test on triggered orders**: about 1,100 flagged orders per arm detect a 20% relative drop in the flagged size-return rate.
4. **Decision rule:** scale a category if at least about 25% of viewers follow the tip, the flagged size-return rate falls by 20% or more, and sales on tipped pages fall by no more than 2%. Stop or raise the cut-off if the net value per tipped order is negative.
5. **Fit the review classifier on platform tags**, not public data, and show review-based tips only where a product has informative reviews. For products without reviews rely on history and brand fit.
6. **KPIs:** size-return rate per category, exchange rate, tip follow rate, add-to-cart on tipped pages, calibration error by month, share of orders on products with no reviews, and the reason-code noise rate from a sampled audit."""
